
\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\textbf{Asymmetry of the comparison.} The hypothesis agent knows the rule families in advance; the learned agents do not, and primitive PPO gets a far smaller budget. The performance gaps are therefore statements about agents under these particular information and budget conditions. The matched-information control is the most informative result precisely because it removes part of the asymmetry, and the primitive-PPO failure should not be taken as evidence that deep reinforcement learning fails in general.

\textbf{What drives performance.} Three things separate the agents. The first is the macro-action structure: every agent that chooses among testable macro-actions is far above the reactive agent and primitive PPO. The second is evidence tracking: memory and revision are necessary, and Proposition~\ref{prop:one}, an analytical check, predicts the failed tests that elimination needs in both families. The third is how well beliefs are available when choosing: posterior sampling matches the hypothesis agent until difficulty 4--5, where the information bonus and expected-value reward explain the gap, and learned agents match it once they are shown the beliefs. The matched-information control suggests that what the learned macro agents lack is the belief state, not the capacity to use it.

\textbf{Where learning falls short, and what it does not show.} Without belief inputs, learned agents solve their training distributions but not unseen color symbols or larger vault codes. The belief-augmented agent does not have the color problem, but that is because the supplied features are symbol-free; this says nothing about whether a network could learn such features from raw observations.

\textbf{Costs of structure.} Hard assumptions are cheap when true and expensive when false. The soft exclusivity prior removed the master-key failure here, but only because the violation is detectable (a second door opens with a key already used). It is adaptive to detectable violations and nothing more: an assumption whose violation never shows up in the feedback could not be repaired this way.

\textbf{Limitations.} Both families are synthetic and, apart from the jammed-lock test, deterministic and fully observable; the benchmarks use no real-world data and support no claim about scientific discovery, robotics or other real settings. The hypothesis spaces are hand-specified, all macro-level agents receive a hand-built action vocabulary, and the generic skills are hand-built too. The hypothesis agent's templates match the generators, which reactive and primitive agents do not know (PSRL and the macro agents share them). The number of independently trained models differs by cell: @{NM('macro_gru','main')} each for Macro-FF/GRU on the mixed-difficulty distribution and @{NM('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation')} on regime D; @{NM('macro_gruB')} for Macro-GRU+belief; @{NM('macro_gru','B_compositional')}, @{NM('macro_gru','C_chain_length')} and @{NM('macro_gru','E_novel_combination')} on regimes B, C and E; @{NM('macro_gru','A_interpolation')} and @{NM('macro_gru','C2_size_only')} on A and C2; @{NGENERIC} for the generic-skill agents (no inference reported); and one for primitive PPO, which is also under-trained. Intervals in the cells with few runs are correspondingly unreliable. Perfect scores come from finite samples and say nothing about reliability beyond them. The constants $V,\mu,\alpha,\beta$ were set by hand, and steps-to-success is subject to survivor bias. We study no language or foundation models, and the CausalGame check covers only the benchmark's survival score, with agents that are generic surrogate-model searchers rather than the templated hypothesis agent of the grid.

\section{Conclusion}
In two structurally different benchmarks, agents that track evidence and test hypotheses came close to the failed-test counts predicted by an elementary proposition, and they extrapolated to unseen color symbols and larger vault codes where learned policies without beliefs did not. A learned agent given the tracker's beliefs closed most of the gap at the hardest levels and on unseen colors, which narrows the claim: the benefit is mainly in what the agent is told about its own uncertainty. A hard exclusivity assumption was catastrophic when false, and a soft, self-correcting version removed the problem in our tests, for violations that the feedback can reveal. Under jammed-lock noise, posterior sampling degraded faster than hypothesis testing at difficulty 3, while at difficulty 4 all agents lost a similar amount. The EV+IG agent's advantage did not carry over to CausalGame, where no hypothesis space is supplied. All of this holds for synthetic, mostly deterministic benchmarks with hand-built interfaces, and the primitive-PPO baseline is under-trained. Open questions are whether the belief features can be learned from raw observations, whether the skills can be learned from primitives, and whether the conclusions survive noise and partial observability.

